00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:11.020
Leah: For a product to be successfully used,
a good ‘user interface’ [UI] is critical.

00:00:11.509 --> 00:00:16.110
Users won’t stay long and will quickly stop
using a product such as a website or app

00:00:16.209 --> 00:00:18.369
if they are having a bad user experience.

00:00:18.889 --> 00:00:23.279
When users leave a site quickly this is
known as having a ‘high bounce rate’.

00:00:23.759 --> 00:00:30.900
Bad UI design could also result in security issues,
data integrity, and affect the performance of a website.

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:37.610
Chris: One of the main overarching considerations when
creating, is to put the user at the heart of the design.

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:44.430
For the user to have a good experience, the UI designer
will need to focus on certain elements, such as:

00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:47.279
• Placing users in control of the interface

00:00:47.990 --> 00:00:50.140
• Making it comfortable to interact with

00:00:50.870 --> 00:00:52.690
• Reducing the cognitive load

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:55.780
• Making user interfaces consistent

00:00:55.839 --> 00:00:59.100
Leah: so, what exactly are the Principles of UI design

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:05.209
When it comes to user interface [UI] design
there are certain characteristics to consider:

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:11.409
• Clarity – All UI element need to be clear
for users to interpretate correctly.

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:18.050
Users should not need to decode a particular meaning
of an element such as interactive and navigation items.

00:01:18.330 --> 00:01:25.089
• Chris: Consistency –The UI design needs to consistent
such as the branding colours and typography.

00:01:25.089 --> 00:01:29.889
This is so users can recognise ways to use
elements such as the placement and colour of a

00:01:29.890 --> 00:01:35.250
button or menu item is always the same colour,
size, font and positioned in the same place.

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:44.410
• Leah: Ease of use – also categorised as user control – the
interface needs to be intuitive and minimises confusion

00:01:44.570 --> 00:01:52.140
such as using simple navigation, providing feedback [such
as a ‘success’ message], and using clear labels for buttons.

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:58.450
• Chris: Comfort – also categorised as Familiarity
this links to clarity and ease of use.

00:01:59.070 --> 00:02:06.289
The UI should allow for previous experiences of the
users, use simple language, include inclusivity, and

00:02:06.299 --> 00:02:12.450
be designed for responsiveness for use on different
items such as a mobile phone, tablet or laptop.

00:02:13.299 --> 00:02:19.939
• Leah: Tolerance – also categorised as Forgiveness
– Users needs to be forgiven of their mistakes that

00:02:19.939 --> 00:02:22.779
they make especially when first using the interface.

00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:28.319
• Chris: Efficiency – the interface should plan
for minimal input and provide shortcuts for

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:31.070
experienced users so they can be more productive.

00:02:31.969 --> 00:02:37.359
• Leah: Accessibility – this is linked to the
Comfort principle as it is key that user

00:02:37.529 --> 00:02:42.480
feel comfortable and in control when using a
product, no matter what their requirements are.

00:02:43.450 --> 00:02:49.369
UI design needs to take motor, cognitive,
hearing, or visual impairments into consideration.

00:02:50.260 --> 00:02:56.989
Chris: Other ‘Principle’ terms that may be used could
include Structure, Simplicity, Visibility, and Feedback.

00:02:57.559 --> 00:03:03.070
This would depend on the type of interface that is
going to be designed, and the strategy, processes,

00:03:03.130 --> 00:03:05.730
or practices of the company or organisation.

